## Why This Is Asked
Tests at mobile viewport ensure responsive UI behavior; verifying data seeding and isolation prevents flaky cross-test interactions; skeleton loaders verification catches performance UX issues during data fetch.

## Key Concepts
- Per-run data seeding and isolation
- Mobile-first E2E flow: search, filter, add-to-cart, checkout
- Skeleton/loading state verification
- API interception/mocking for payments

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright skeleton (mobile viewport, data seed, flow steps)
test('mobile catalog E2E', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.setViewportSize({ width: 375, height: 812 });
  // seed data
  const user = await seedTestUser();
  await page.goto('https://shop.example.test');
  await page.fill('[data-test="search-input"]', 'lamp');
  await page.press('[data-test="search-input"]', 'Enter');
  await page.click('[data-test="price-filter-20-50"]');
  await page.click('[data-test="product-card-0"]');
  await page.click('[data-test="add-to-cart"]');
  await page.click('[data-test="checkout"]');
  await expect(page.locator('[data-test="order-total"]')).toBeVisible();
  // mock payment
  await page.route('**/payments/token', route => route.fulfill({ status: 200, body: JSON.stringify({ token: 'tok_test' }) }));
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for parallel runs across multiple locales?
- How would you validate that skeleton loaders render correctly under slow network conditions?

## Why This Is Asked

End-to-end tests for multi-tenant data pipelines must validate deterministic inputs, failure scenarios, and dashboard correctness under realistic conditions. These tests challenge system reliability by combining data isolation, scheduled execution, and eventual consistency requirements.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant isolation via headers or subdomains
- Deterministic test data using fixtures and mocks
- Idempotent reruns and retry mechanisms
- Failure injection and eventual consistency verification
- Tooling strategy between Playwright and Cypress

## Code Example

```javascript
// Playwright intercepts and fixtures for deterministic run
const csvPath = path.join(__dirname, 'fixtures', 'pipeline_input.csv');
await page.route('https://api.example.com/data', (route) => {
  route.fulfill({
    status: 200,
    contentType: 'application/json',
    body: JSON.stringify({ records: mockApiResponse })
  });
});

// Upload CSV fixture and configure pipeline
await page.setInputFiles('input[type="file"]', csvPath);
await page.click('[data-testid="create-pipeline"]');
await page.fill('[data-testid="pipeline-name"]', 'nightly-analytics');
await page.selectOption('[data-testid="schedule"]', 'nightly');
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests across multiple regions and orchestration layers are common in cloud-native, multi-tenant platforms. This checks data isolation, regional consistency, rollback safety, and observability.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-region provisioning and consistency
- Test data isolation and tenant-scoped seeds
- Idempotent retries and rollback guarantees
- Chaos/traffic shaping for regional failover
- Observability with distributed tracing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo test sketch (Playwright/Cypress style)
test('onboard tenant end-to-end', async () => {
  const tenantId = `tn-${Date.now()}`;
  await signupTenant(tenantId);
  await provisionRegions(tenantId, ['us-east', 'eu-west', 'ap-south']);
  await configureIAM(tenantId, 'default-role');
  await activateBillingTrial(tenantId, 30);
  await assertRegionalConvergence(tenantId, 120000);
  await retryWithIdempotency(tenantId);
  await verifyRollbackOnFailure(tenantId);
  const trace = await fetchTrace(tenantId);
  expect(trace).toBeDefined();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure and mitigate flaky tests in this setup?
- What telemetry and retries would you implement to differentiate real failures from transient regional outages?

